{"reference":"Luke 18","book":"Luke","chapter":18,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Prayer, humility, and mercy","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The opening parables address different but compatible postures in prayer. The persistent widow encourages continued prayer while naming the reality of delayed justice; because the judge is explicitly unrighteous, the contrast does not portray God as abusive or require survivors to endure endless institutional neglect. The Pharisee and tax collector then critiques self-exalting comparison and commends a humble appeal for mercy. It cannot be used to justify contempt for Pharisees, Jews, religious practice, or whole communities.","verse_refs":["Luke 18:1-8","Luke 18:9-14"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_luke_018_f7742e510487e50f4d6446e5"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}